    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`). There's only one test class in the project (the acceptance test), and it's green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit: creating an owner is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100 or more owners have already been registered today (by `registrationDate`).\n\nThe logic is placed at the layer where each concern belongs, mirroring the existing `isCityAtCapacity` pattern:\n\n- **`OwnerRepository`** \u2014 declared `countByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)`, with implementations in all three profiles:\n  - `SpringDataOwnerRepository` (the profile the tests use): a Spring Data derived query.\n  - `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl`: a JPQL `COUNT` query.\n  - `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl`: a SQL `COUNT` on `registration_date`.\n- **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `isDailyOwnerLimitReached()`, gated by a `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT = 100` constant (same style as the existing `CITY_CAPACITY` constant), comparing today's count against the limit.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added the check at the top of creation, returning `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` when the limit is reached.\n\nThe `>= 100` boundary matches the spec (\"100 or more\"), and since new owners default their `registrationDate` to `LocalDate.now()` on persist (existing `@PrePersist` logic), counting by today's date correctly captures owners created today.",
